  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.28.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.27.1 and v5.25.1. v5.28.0 matters to this distribution more than any release since the fork question was settled, because it is the one that stops the engine name from reaching a DARQ disk. On-disk artifact names now derive from identity.json rather than being literals, so the system prompt, the skill-registry subtree with its binary and module, the env contract, the three plugin files with their exported symbols and the notifier npm package all carry this product name instead of the engine one. The accepted_residue register in rebrand.json shrinks accordingly and must be pruned to match, because an entry that no longer occurs is not harmless: it stays a standing permission for the leak to come back without failing the check. Two entries stay on purpose. The Zellij state directories under config and cache keep the engine name because they live outside the CLI configuration directory, no journal claims them, and deriving them would silently orphan whatever state the person already has. The playwright lockfile root name is neutral now, so that one goes. One migration consequence to expect on an existing DARQ installation: the artifacts with the old names are retired and the new ones created in the same run, which works because v5.28.0 also fixes a defect where an id scheme change deleted a file instead of replacing it, but the directory the old name held is left empty rather than pruned, because pruning only removes directories an installation recorded creating and an installation from before v5.28.0 recorded none."
  }
}
